    "narrative": "Today\u2019s lineup is anchored by **James Wood** (78.42) and **Mickey Moniak** (75.75), who represent our strongest offensive upside against favorable matchups. **James Wood** is in an elite spot atop the order against Chandler; his 0.420 xwOBA profile and high-leverage leadoff opportunity against a 4.92 xERA arm make him our primary engine for Runs and RBI. Similarly, **Mickey Moniak** benefits from a massive 14% park factor boost and a plus-matchup against Tyler Mahle, combining excellent peripheral metrics with a favorable batting order position that optimizes his chance to drive in base runners. **Brice Turang** (73.40) also earns a strong start, with his speed profile receiving a 4% boost due to the slow delivery of Eduardo Rodriguez, providing a critical catalyst for our scarce Stolen Base category.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Cole Young** (48.48), who hovered near the threshold of our marginal tier but was ultimately squeezed out due to a negative park factor against Trey Yesavage and a lack of confirmed lineup placement. While **Cole Young** possesses the contact profile to contribute, the optimizer favored the higher ceiling of **Max Muncy** (53.64) and **Alex Freeland** (53.77), whose matchups against Eury P\u00e9rez and JP Sears respectively offer better paths to scoring production. Risk management remains a priority: the lineup is heavily dependent on assumed batting orders for **Dalton Rushing**, **Gabriel Moreno**, **Josh Naylor**, **Max Muncy**, **Alex Freeland**, **Henry Bolte**, and **Corbin Carroll**. Any late downward shifts in the order for these players\u2014particularly for **Dalton Rushing** or **Alex Freeland**\u2014will necessitate a re-evaluation of their efficiency scores. Furthermore, **Seiya Suzuki** (64.03) must navigate a 5% penalty for head-on wind, though his platoon advantage over the opposing lefty keeps him safely in the strong start category.",
